; SPDX-License-Identifier: 0BSD
; naught.source.vla - Naught: Vel et Aut (CART.9), a bench of logic gates: the engine's
; first irregular graph, every gate a formula of the cells it reads. The pass expands this
; file into naught.vla beside it, the rows the engine loads (docs/SPEC.md section 7.8):
; edit this one, then expand it again with
;   cargo run --release --example expand
; Its bench, the block near its end, is written by
;   cargo run --release --example naught
; and never by hand.
;
; The bench. Row 1 names its columns, the switches' letters and the gates' Latin names. A
; 16-bit ripple adder stands from A1, a bit a row from row 3: its switches a and b in
; columns A and B, its carry-in at F2, its five gates a bit in C to G, the sum's bits in G
; and the carry out of its last bit in F18. An 8 by 8 multiplier stands from I1: its
; switches a along row 2 from J2 and b down column I from I4, its partial products in
; J4:Q11, and each of its other roles a block of nine columns to their right, a row of
; carry-save adders a row and its last row the ripple that ends the product. Every cell
; holds 0 or 1, and a 1 is drawn in Lovelace Lavender. Select a switch and type 1 or 0,
; then Enter: the palette opens holding the switch's row,
;   (cell "Bench" "A3" 1)
; Enter writes it, and every gate the switch reaches computes in the act's frame. The
; Lesson, the sheet a load shows first, says the rest to whoever loads it.
;
; The game. Fifteen levels stand between the Lesson and the Bench, a sheet each, from NON
; built of NON-ET alone to a toy CPU that runs a program of its ROM sheet (CART.9's third
; and fourth slices): a level's switches, its flip-flops from X on, the room for a person's
; gates, its outputs, its lamps, the output due, and its words; T tests every input of it,
; an input a settle step, and its gates are counted against a budget and the least known,
; each read by its formula's text: a gate of a shape outside the set the level gives holds
; a pass to one star. Their keys are the game's, and the Bench's and the ROM's a person's
; (docs/SPEC.md section 7.2, version 6's (game ...) and (work ...)).
;
; The gates, in Latin, each arithmetic over 0 and 1 and each a macro below:
;   NON, not: 1 less its input.            ET, and: the product of its two.
;   VEL, or: the greater of its two.       AUT, either but not both: their sum's parity.
;   NON-ET, not both: Sheffer's stroke.    NEC, neither: Peirce's arrow.
; Latin is said to keep two ors, vel the inclusive and aut the exclusive. The Stanford
; Encyclopedia of Philosophy's entry of 2016, Ray Jennings and Andrew Hartline's, called
; that a myth in its section 7, and its revision of 2024 says that aut has inclusive uses;
; the bench keeps both anyway.
;
; How it settles. The cartridge says (settle 65536): after an act the engine steps the bench
; again, with no act, until a frame changes nothing (docs/SPEC.md section 5, version 6). At
; full rate every gate a switch reaches computes in the act's own frame, so an act is two
; frames, its own and one that proves nothing moved; the load settles too, so the bench
; opens on its values. The frame floors build their benches without it: a settle numbers
; the Clock by acts, and their switches read the Clock as the frame.
;
; What it measures (docs/REARVIEW.md, CART.9's scoping). A gate reads cells anywhere, so
; the bench is a graph of formulas, its depth the adder's carry rippling through every
; bit. The frame floors build the same bench larger, a 4,096-bit adder and a 64 by 64
; multiplier, twice: laid by fill, each role one shape, and shuffled among the same cells,
; every gate its own shape. What the second costs over the first is the regularity
; premium.

(cartridge "naught"
  (spec 6)                          ; the version of docs/SPEC.md this cartridge is written to: (settle K), (bound ...), (game ...), (read ...) and (work ...)
  (title "Naught: Vel et Aut")      ; what the host shows: the gates' own words
  (rate 0)                          ; an act, an edit or a press, steps the bench; nothing else does
  (screen grid 80 50)               ; no Screen: the host shows the Lesson, the first visible sheet
  (seed 1)                          ; nothing on the bench is drawn from it
  (settle 65536)                    ; an act's settle, at most 65,536 steps: at full rate, one
  (bound "Lesson" "I NON" "II ET" "III VEL" "IV AUT" "V ELIGE" "VI DISTRIBUE" "VII SEMIS" "VIII SUMMA" "IX QUATTUOR" "X MORA" "XI TENE" "XII MEMORIA" "XIII NUMERA" "XIV RATIO" "XV MACHINA" "ROM" "Bench")  ; the lesson, the levels and the bench, each drawn to its extent and no further
  (read "Lesson")                   ; the lesson, read-only: no key or row of a person's writes it
  (work "ROM" "Bench")              ; the program and the bench, a person's to work: every key typed there is a cell's
  (game "I NON" "II ET" "III VEL" "IV AUT" "V ELIGE" "VI DISTRIBUE" "VII SEMIS" "VIII SUMMA" "IX QUATTUOR" "X MORA" "XI TENE" "XII MEMORIA" "XIII NUMERA" "XIV RATIO" "XV MACHINA")  ; the levels: their keys are the game's, T, N, P, R, Space, 1, 0, C and G
  (author "Spreadsheet Company")    ; who wrote it
  (license "0BSD")                  ; the header's twin: copy this file to start a bench of your own
  (notes "Naught: Vel et Aut, a bench of logic gates in Latin: NON, ET, VEL, AUT, NON-ET and NEC. A 16-bit ripple adder from A1 and an 8 by 8 multiplier from I1, every gate a formula of the cells it reads, every 1 in lavender. Select a switch and type 1 or 0, then Enter, (cell \"Bench\" \"A3\" 1), and every gate it reaches computes; the Lesson says the rest."))

; ---- the gates, each a cell or a fill of one formula -----------------------------------
; A gate is written by its Latin name, its cell or its range, then each of its inputs as
; the range's first cell reads it: (aut "C3:C18" "A3" "B3") is the parity of a and b on
; every row from 3 to 18. A gate's value is 0 or 1, a number, so a bus is a sum of its
; bits and a look draws each value. Since KIT.5 each gate is an expression the formula
; syntax writes; an input is the text the generator writes, "A3", made the reference A3 by
; symbol, so the bench's block below stands as its generator wrote it. A gate writes its
; row itself, through formula-expand and never the library's rule, since the frame floors
; build the bench from this source with the lesson, the levels and the include cut out.

(defmacro (non at x)
  "NON, not: 1 less its input"
  (formula "Bench" at (formula-expand (- 1 (symbol x)))))

(defmacro (et at x y)
  "ET, and: the product of its two inputs"
  (formula "Bench" at (formula-expand (* (symbol x) (symbol y)))))

(defmacro (vel at x y)
  "VEL, or: the greater of its two inputs"
  (formula "Bench" at (formula-expand (max (symbol x) (symbol y)))))

(defmacro (aut at x y)
  "AUT, either but not both: the parity of its inputs' sum"
  (formula "Bench" at (formula-expand (mod (+ (symbol x) (symbol y)) 2))))

(defmacro (non-et at x y)
  "NON-ET, not both, Sheffer's stroke: 1 less the product of its inputs"
  (formula "Bench" at (formula-expand (- 1 (* (symbol x) (symbol y))))))

(defmacro (nec at x y)
  "NEC, neither, Peirce's arrow: 1 less the greater of its inputs"
  (formula "Bench" at (formula-expand (- 1 (max (symbol x) (symbol y))))))

; A command's test, named once for the levels and the Lab.
(defmacro (a-command word)
  "the command named by the word, run at this frame: Input!$B$20=\"word\""
  (= Input!$B$20 word))

; ---- the lesson beside the bench: the floors build their benches without it ----
; What the game teaches, on a sheet of its own, the first a load shows, bound to its extent and read-only: a line a row
; in a column wide enough to hold it. Its own sheet keeps the Bench's cells numbers alone, which the language reads as
; arrays.
(sheet "Lesson" visible)
(column "Lesson" "A" 140 shown none)
(cell "Lesson" "A1" "Naught: Vel et Aut")
(cell "Lesson" "A2" "Logic gates in Latin, and fifteen levels from one gate to a machine: NON, ET, VEL and AUT, sums, a clock, a memory, a counter.")
(cell "Lesson" "A3" "The levels are the sheets after this one: a tab, or Ctrl+Alt+PageDown (Ctrl+PageDown in full screen), and Ctrl+Alt+PageUp back.")
(cell "Lesson" "A5" "On a level, Space toggles the switch the selection stands on, 1 and 0 set it, a click toggles it. A lamp is lit where its output is 1.")
(cell "Lesson" "A6" "Write the gates in the room, C to I, and the outputs in J: type = on a cell, and the palette opens its formula. Enter writes it.")
(cell "Lesson" "A7" "Enter on a cell opens its formula as it stands, F2 too, and Escape leaves it. Delete empties a cell; Tab moves right. On a room's cell with no gate, 1 and 0 write themselves.")
(cell "Lesson" "A8" "T tests every input, an input a frame, and stops at the first a lamp gets wrong, the board standing on it. R sets the switches to 0.")
(cell "Lesson" "A9" "N and P show the next and the previous level. A pass is a star; within the budget, two; at the least known, three, until the next test.")
(cell "Lesson" "A11" "The gates, in Latin: NON, not, =1-a. ET, and, =a*b. VEL, or, =MAX(a,b). AUT, either but not both, =MOD(a+b,2).")
(cell "Lesson" "A12" "NON-ET, not both, =1-a*b, Sheffer's stroke. NEC, neither, =1-MAX(a,b), Peirce's arrow. Either alone builds every other gate.")
(cell "Lesson" "A13" "Level I gives NON-ET alone; each later level, the gates proven before it, and a wire, =a. Any other shape caps a pass at one star.")
(cell "Lesson" "A14" "A gate reads the switches, the room's cells, 0 or 1. The reckoning names the first gate outside the set; the verdict, an error's cell.")
(cell "Lesson" "A15" "From X on, a flip-flop stands in A below the switches: it holds its value, and at a tick takes its row of J, as J stood. C ticks.")
(cell "Lesson" "A16" "G clears the flip-flops and runs the clock until they stand still, or have spent every state they can hold: then it never halts.")
(cell "Lesson" "A17" "T tests J at every input and every state: a machine right for one tick from every state is right at every tick, by induction.")
(cell "Lesson" "A18" "From X, ELIGE is given too, =IF(s,b,a), proven at V. XV MACHINA runs the program on the sheet ROM, a line a word, typed there.")
(cell "Lesson" "A19" "After the levels and the ROM, the Bench, a sandbox where every key types: a 16-bit adder from A1 and an 8 by 8 multiplier from I1.")
(cell "Lesson" "A20" "The adder: a in A3:A18 and b in B3:B18, low bit first, its carry-in F2; its sum G3:G18, its carry out F18.")
(cell "Lesson" "A21" "Each bit adds three: C = AUT(a,b), D = ET(a,b), E = ET(C,carry), F = VEL(D,E) the carry on, G = AUT(C,carry) the sum.")
(cell "Lesson" "A22" "The multiplier: a in J2:Q2 and b in I4:I11, low bit first; its partial products ET(a,b) in J4:Q11, summed in rows.")
(cell "Lesson" "A23" "Its product, low bit first: J4, S5, BC6:BC11, S12, BD12:BI12 and AZ12.")
(cell "Lesson" "A24" "Try it: a all ones, A3:A18, then the carry-in F2 to 1: 65,535 + 1 ripples through all sixteen bits in one act. 255 x 255 = 65,025.")
(cell "Lesson" "A25" "Delete empties a cell, Backspace opens it emptied: an empty text is no number, so the gates it feeds say #VALUE!. Ex nihilo nihil fit.")
(cell "Lesson" "A27" "Tertium non datur: a cell holds 0 or 1, and no third, Delete aside. Latin is said to keep two ors, vel and aut; the Stanford")
(cell "Lesson" "A28" "Encyclopedia of Philosophy called that a myth in 2016: aut has inclusive uses too. Naught keeps both anyway.")
; ---- the lesson's end ----

; ---- the levels: the game, between the lesson and the bench; the floors build their benches without them ----
; A level a sheet (CART.9's third slice, decisions 16 to 28, and its fourth, decisions 34 to 48), bound to its extent: its
; title in row 1 and its heads in row 2; its switches in column A from row 3, each named beside it in B; the room for its
; gates, C to I, and its outputs in J, each named in K, which a person writes as formulas, = opening a cell's edit; a lamp
; a row in L, lit while its output is 1; the output due in M, the specification's at the switches standing; and its words
; in N, its reckoning and its test's verdict in N8 and N9. Its keys are the game's, the manifest's (game ...): Space
; toggles the switch the selection stands on, 1 and 0 set it, and write themselves on a cell of the room that holds no
; gate, and a click toggles it; T tests every input; R sets the
; switches to 0; N and P show the next and the previous level. The Lab keeps the test and each level's reckoning, the
; Gates sheet reads each gate of its room by its formula's text, the Camera shows the level N and P move to, and the
; Commands sheet holds the keys.
;
; The clocked levels (decisions 34 to 48). A flip-flop stands in column A below a level's switches, a switch that also
; takes its row of J, as J stood, at a tick of the clock: C ticks once, and G clears the flip-flops and runs the clock
; until they stand still or have spent every state they can hold. No cell ticks but by an act, so that a person's keys
; never wait on a counter. T treats a flip-flop as a switch, its bit the input's: J is the next state, proven at every
; state and every input, and a machine right for one tick from every state is right at every tick, by induction. XV's
; word stands above its flip-flops, the line of the ROM sheet its counter names.
(include "../common.vla")

(format 2 "A080E0" general nowrap)  ; a lamp lit, the lamps' lavender

(defmacro (level-sheet sheet names)
  "a level's sheet: its columns, B as wide as its switches' names, a 1 lit in the gates' lavender and a lamp lit in the lamps'"
  (column sheet "A" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "B" names shown none)
  (column sheet "C" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "D" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "E" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "F" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "G" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "H" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "I" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "J" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "K" 6 shown none)
  (column sheet "L" 5 shown none)
  (column sheet "M" 4 shown none)
  (column sheet "N" 128 shown none)
  (look sheet 1 1)
  (look sheet "●" 2))

(defmacro (level-heads sheet title)
  "a level's title in A1, over the empty row to its right, and its columns' heads in row 2"
  (cell sheet "A1" title)
  (cell sheet "A2" "in")
  (cell sheet "C2" "the room")
  (cell sheet "J2" "out")
  (cell sheet "L2" "lamp")
  (cell sheet "M2" "due"))

; ---- a switch, a flip-flop and a word line, each its own last value ----

(defmacro (on-this-level word sheet)
  "the command named by the word, run at this frame on the sheet"
  (and (a-command word) (= Input!$B$19 sheet)))

(defmacro (on-this-switch sheet)
  "an act of this frame standing on this cell of the sheet's column A"
  (and (= Input!$B$19 sheet) (= Input!$B$11 1) (= Input!$B$12 (row))))

(defmacro (of-level column lab-row)
  "a level's cell of a column of the Lab: its testing column at row 11 is Lab!$N$11"
  (ref "Lab" (&expand "$" column "$" lab-row)))

(defmacro (its-last sheet first)
  "this cell's twin, the first cell of a range as the range's first reads it"
  (ref (&expand sheet ".last") first))

(defmacro (test-bit i)
  "bit i of the input's number, i from 1: what switch i takes while T runs its level"
  (mod (int (/ Lab!$B$6 (choose i 1 2 4 8 16 32 64 128 256 512 1024))) 2))

(defmacro (test-hash r)
  "the hash of the switch of row r and the input's number, the bench's stimulus at column A"
  (mod (+ (* r 40503) 9973 (* Lab!$B$6 65519)) 65521))

(defmacro (test-draw r)
  "a bit drawn by lot for the switch of row r at the input's number: the bench's stimulus at column A"
  (mod (int (/ (mod (* (test-hash r) (test-hash r)) 65521) 256)) 2))

(defmacro (by-hand own)
  "a switch at a person's act on it, own its last value: 1, 0, or toggled by Space or a click, else as it was"
  (priority
    ((a-command "one") 1)
    ((a-command "zero") 0)
    ((or (a-command "toggle") (= Input!$B$13 1)) (- 1 own))
    (else own)))

(defmacro (level-switches sheet lab-row range)
  "a level's switches, each its own last value: 0 at R on its level; while T runs its level, the bit of the input's number
   that the switch's row names; and otherwise Space, 1, 0 or a click on it. lab-row, the level's row of the Lab"
  (rule sheet range
    (priority
      ((on-this-level "reset" sheet) 0)
      ((= (of-level "N" lab-row) 1) (test-bit (- (row) 2)))
      ((on-this-switch sheet) (by-hand (its-last sheet (first-of range))))
      (else (its-last sheet (first-of range))))))

(defmacro (level-flip-flops sheet lab-row range next the-bit)
  "a level's flip-flops: a switch that also takes next, its row of J as J stood, at a tick; 0 at R and at G on its level,
   so that a run begins from nothing; and the-bit while T runs it, the state a test proves J at. J holding no number gives
   0, so that a tick carries no error around the loop"
  (rule sheet range
    (priority
      ((or (on-this-level "reset" sheet) (on-this-level "run" sheet)) 0)
      ((= (of-level "N" lab-row) 1) the-bit)
      ((= (of-level "I" lab-row) 1) (iferror (n (its-last sheet next)) 0))
      ((on-this-switch sheet) (by-hand (its-last sheet (first-of range))))
      (else (its-last sheet (first-of range))))))

(defmacro (level-word sheet lab-row range)
  "XV's word: the bits of the ROM's line its counter names, Lab!$E$8, low bit first; the input's bits while T runs it; and,
   after a test that failed, as they stood until the clock moves or R, so that the board stands on the input that failed"
  (rule sheet range
    (priority
      ((= (of-level "N" lab-row) 1) (test-bit (- (row) 2)))
      ((and (= (of-level "B" lab-row) 2) (= (of-level "I" lab-row) 0)) (its-last sheet (first-of range)))
      (else (mod (int (/ Lab!$E$8 (choose (- (row) 2) 1 2 4 8 16 32 64))) 2)))))

(defmacro (level-lamps sheet range)
  "a level's lamps, a dot lit in the lamps' lavender while the output to its left is 1, a ring otherwise, and a question
   mark while it holds an error, which is neither"
  (rule sheet range (iferror (if (= J3 1) "●" "○") "?")))

; ---- a level's words ----

(defmacro (level-reckoning sheet lab-row budget least)
  "a level's reckoning in N8: its gates, how many stand outside the set it gives and the first of them, its budget and
   the least known, and its last test's stars. lab-row, the level's row of the Lab"
  (rule sheet "N8"
    (& "Gates " (ref "Lab" (&expand "E" lab-row))
       (if (> (ref "Lab" (&expand "Q" lab-row)) 0)
           (& ", " (ref "Lab" (&expand "Q" lab-row)) " outside the set given, the first at " (ref "Lab" (&expand "R" lab-row)))
           "")
       (&expand "; the budget " budget ", the least known " least ": ") (ref "Lab" (&expand "H" lab-row)))))

(defmacro (proven inputs)
  "the verdict of a test that tried every input"
  (&expand "Quod erat demonstrandum: every one of its " inputs " inputs passes."))

(defmacro (error-kind lab-row)
  "the kind of the room's first error, its place among the language's eight: ERROR.TYPE's 1 to 7, and 8 for its 14"
  (min (ref "Lab" (&expand "X" lab-row)) 8))

(defmacro (error-spelled lab-row)
  "the room's first error as Excel spells it"
  (choose (error-kind lab-row) "#NULL!" "#DIV/0!" "#VALUE!" "#REF!" "#NAME?" "#NUM!" "#N/A" "#CALC!"))

(defmacro (error-cause lab-row)
  "what makes the room's first error, in the cell or in a cell it reads"
  (choose (error-kind lab-row)
    "meets two ranges that do not cross."
    "divides by zero."
    "takes a text for a number, and an emptied cell holds one."
    "names a cell that is not there."
    "names what the language does not know."
    "makes a number too large for a cell."
    "finds no value."
    "calculates no answer."))

(defmacro (an-error-at lab-row)
  "the room's first cell holding an error, named with its kind and what makes it"
  (& (ref "Lab" (&expand "S" lab-row))
     " holds "
     (error-spelled lab-row)
     ": it or a cell it reads "
     (error-cause lab-row)))

(defmacro (why-it-stopped lab-row)
  "what the board shows where a test stopped: an error's cell, a switch written over that did not follow the test, or a lamp"
  (priority
    ((<> (ref "Lab" (&expand "S" lab-row)) "") (an-error-at lab-row))
    ((= (ref "Lab" (&expand "L" lab-row)) 0) "a switch written over does not follow the test. Load restores it, and empties every level.")
    (else "a lamp differs from its due.")))

(defmacro (level-verdict sheet lab-row inputs passed)
  "a level's test's verdict in N9: counting the inputs while T runs, where it stopped and why, the room's first cell that
   holds an error, and passed, the words of a pass"
  (rule sheet "N9"
    (priority
      ((= (ref "Lab" (&expand "N" lab-row)) 1) (& "Calculemus: input " (+ Lab!$B$6 1) (&expand " of " inputs)))
      ((= (ref "Lab" (&expand "B" lab-row)) 2)
       (& "The test stops at input " (+ (ref "Lab" (&expand "C" lab-row)) 1) (&expand " of " inputs ", standing on the board: ") (why-it-stopped lab-row)))
      ((<> (ref "Lab" (&expand "S" lab-row)) "") (& (an-error-at lab-row) " Ex nihilo nihil fit."))
      ((= (ref "Lab" (&expand "B" lab-row)) 1) passed)
      (else "T tests every input, an input a frame. Calculemus!"))))

(defmacro (level-keys sheet at)
  "the keys of a level, said where it has room; and below its verdict, in N10, what a write over the cartridge's own cells
   costs (decision 23): a formula enters by the load or a person's write, and R writes values"
  (cell sheet at "Space toggles the switch the selection stands on, 1 and 0 set it, a click toggles it; in the room 1 and 0 write themselves; T tests, R resets, N and P move.")
  (cell sheet "N10" "A switch or a lamp written over is lost until Load, which loads every level afresh: R writes values, never a formula."))

(defmacro (clock-keys)
  "what a clocked level says of its clock before a run"
  "C ticks the clock once. G clears the flip-flops and runs the clock until they stand still.")

(defmacro (level-clock sheet lab-row holding limit)
  "a clocked level's clock in N11: its keys; the tick a run stands at; where a run came to rest; or, after limit ticks that
   each moved a flip-flop, that it never rests. holding, how many flip-flops hold how many states' first words, and limit
   those states: a machine that has moved at limit ticks has stood in some state twice (decision 37). A level's run alone
   speaks: Lab!$E$2 names the level last run"
  (rule sheet "N11"
    (priority
      ((<> Lab!$E$2 (-expand lab-row 10)) (clock-keys))
      ((= Lab!$E$3 1) (& "Currit, it runs: tick " Lab!$E$4))
      ((= Lab!$E$9 1) (& "Stat, it stands: its last move at tick " (- Lab!$E$4 1) "."))
      ((= Lab!$E$9 2) (&expand "In aeternum: " limit " ticks, no rest. " holding " " limit " states, so one came twice: it never halts. A short tape answers Turing."))
      (else (clock-keys)))))

; ---- I NON: not, from NON-ET alone ----
(sheet "I NON" visible)
(level-sheet "I NON" 3)
(level-heads "I NON" "I NON: not, from NON-ET alone")
(cells "I NON" ("B3" "a") ("K3" "y"))
(level-switches "I NON" 11 "A3")
(level-lamps "I NON" "L3")
(rules "I NON" ("M3" Lab!B51))
(cells "I NON"
  ("N1" "NON, not: the lamp lit where a is 0, and dark where a is 1.")
  ("N2" "Given NON-ET alone, not both, =1-x*y. Write y in J3, with any gates before it in the room, C to I.")
  ("N3" "A gate's two inputs may be one cell read twice.")
  ("N4" "NON-ET alone builds every gate: Peirce found it about 1880 and never published it, and Sheffer did, in 1913.")
  ("N5" "Tertium non datur: a lamp is lit or it is dark, and there is no third.")
  ("N6" "Ockham's razor, in words found in none of Ockham's extant works: entities are not to be multiplied beyond necessity."))
(level-keys "I NON" "N7")
(level-reckoning "I NON" 11 2 1)
(level-verdict "I NON" 11 2 (proven 2))

; ---- II ET: and, from NON-ET and NON ----
(sheet "II ET" visible)
(level-sheet "II ET" 3)
(level-heads "II ET" "II ET: and, from NON-ET and NON")
(cells "II ET" ("B3" "a") ("B4" "b") ("K3" "y"))
(level-switches "II ET" 12 "A3:A4")
(level-lamps "II ET" "L3")
(rules "II ET" ("M3" Lab!C51))
(cells "II ET"
  ("N1" "ET, and: the lamp lit where a and b are both 1.")
  ("N2" "Given NON-ET, =1-x*y, and NON, proven at I, =1-x. Write y in J3.")
  ("N3" "De Morgan, 1847: not both is the one not or the other not, NON-ET(a,b) = VEL(NON a, NON b). Turn it around."))
(level-keys "II ET" "N7")
(level-reckoning "II ET" 12 3 2)
